// MC-PATCH: maic-doubled-tool-name-recovery-v1
// INSERT-BEFORE: 	const segments = normalizeStringEntries(normalizedDelimiter.split("."));
// Lesson 1423 (2026-10-21, David): David saw this in MC Terminal on the Paid
// tier ("web_searchweb_search"):
//   I can't use the tool "web_searchweb_search" here because it isn't available.
//
// That sentence is generated verbatim by `rewriteUnknownToolLoopMessage()`
// (this file, ~line 9977) from the literal string the model emitted. So the
// model produced the name `web_searchweb_search` — a DOUBLED tool name.
//
// WHY DOUBLED NAMES ARE UNRECOVERABLE (verified against the real bundle):
// `buildStructuredToolNameCandidates()` (this file, ~line 9542) is the only
// repair path. It splits on `/` and `.` and tries every suffix, so a PREFIXED
// name is recoverable but a REPEATED name is not:
//   "functions.web_search"  -> [..., "web_search"]       -> RESOLVES
//   "openclaw.web_search"   -> [..., "web_search"]       -> RESOLVES
//   "web_searchweb_search"  -> ["web_searchweb_search"]  -> NO MATCH
// A doubled name has no delimiter to split on, so it can never be repaired and
// falls straight through to the "unknown tool" message with the doubled string
// verbatim. That is exactly what David saw.
//
// EVIDENCE THAT THE TOOL LIST ITSELF IS CLEAN (ruled out as the cause):
//   - MAIC dedups by function name, server wins (api/routes/chat.py:189-195).
//   - Asked Nemotron Super AND GLM to echo their own tool list, 3x each. Both
//     reported: get_weather / web_search / get_current_time / calculate.
//     The model is never shown a doubled name.
//   - maic-plugin's merge is `model.tools || provider.tools || []` — `||`
//     short-circuits, so it can never concatenate.
// So this is NOT a duplicate-registration problem (that would recreate Lesson
// 1101's registration collision; web_search and web_fetch are already working
// OpenClaw host built-ins). The defect is purely that the repair path cannot
// handle a name which is exactly two identical halves.
//
// WHAT THIS PATCH DOES — additive only:
// If `trimmed` is exactly some string concatenated with ITSELF, also try the
// single half. This flows through the existing allowlist check in
// `resolveStructuredAllowedToolName()`, so it cannot match anything that isn't
// already a registered tool — it only makes an otherwise-unmatchable name
// matchable when the half is a real tool.
//   "web_searchweb_search" -> candidates [..., "web_search"] -> RESOLVES
//
// WHY THIS IS SAFE (verified by replaying 18 names through before/after):
//   - Purely additive: new candidates appended, none removed or reordered.
//   - Only fires on an exact self-concatenation (even length, byte-identical
//     halves), which is not a legal OpenAI/ollama tool name.
//   - Still gated by the allowlist, so no tool name can be invented. e.g.
//     "write_fileread_file" stays unknown because "read_file" is not its half.
//   - Empty and whitespace-only inputs still return no candidates.
//   - Exactly 1 behavior change across the 18-name suite; 0 regressions.
//
// WHY AN INSERT AND NOT A .js.replace:
// patch-openclaw-dist.sh .js.replace is strictly ONE line in / ONE line out
// (the awk keeps only the last non-blank line of each block as the whole
// replacement). This fix needs several statements AND must land OUTSIDE the
// `for (index...)` loop above — inside the loop it would be dead code,
// because a doubled name has no delimiter, produces a single segment, and so
// never enters that loop at all. An earlier attempt using .js.replace silently
// produced exactly that broken result and node --check still passed. Hence:
// .js.insert, sentinel = the `const segments = ...` line that opens the suffix
// loop; that line is unique in the bundle (verified with grep -cF).
//
// OPEN RISK — DELIBERATELY NOT ADDRESSED HERE: this repairs the name AFTER the
// model emits it. It does not explain WHY the model emitted a doubled name
// (candidate trigger: Nemotron Super under `thinking=medium`, which per
// memory/2026-10-12 burns 11-12K reasoning tokens and can return
// `finish_reason=length`). If this patch doesn't fully stop the symptom, that
// root cause needs a separate look. Patching the symptom is the conservative
// first move — it cannot regress anything the allowlist already enforced.
	if (trimmed.length > 1 && trimmed.length % 2 === 0) {
		const half = trimmed.slice(0, trimmed.length / 2);
		if (half === trimmed.slice(half.length)) {
			addCandidate(half);
			addCandidate(normalizeToolName(half));
		}
	}